
%%
%% SECTION 8: SECURITY CONTROLS
%%
\section{Security Controls: Mitigation as Defense in Depth}
\label{sec:controls}

Mitigation research spans the model lifecycle, from curation of preference data through targeted
fine-tuning, inference-time prompting and steering, real-time monitoring, and governance.
Figure~\ref{fig:mitigation} organizes the interventions into four tiers, and
Appendix~\ref{app:mitigations} grades every mitigation the search found. Reading them as security
controls imposes a discipline the literature mostly lacks: each control must state which channel of
the threat model it addresses (Table~\ref{tab:coverage}), what evidence supports it, and which
adversary class it was validated against.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_mitigation.pdf}
\caption{Four control tiers with example mitigations and their evidence grade from
Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison} (A: at least two model families, at least two domains, and
multi-turn or held-out testing; B: two of these; C: one). Later tiers complement but do not
substitute for earlier ones.}
\label{fig:mitigation}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Grading the Evidence}
\label{subsec:grading}

Ordinal effectiveness ratings hide how heterogeneous the underlying evidence is, so
Table~\ref{tab:mitigation_comparison} instead reports each source's own effect verbatim, whether
another group has re-run the method, and a grade for breadth of evidence. Of 36 graded methods, 8
reach A, 12 B, 8 C, and 6 D; two proposed specifically against sycophancy report no sycophancy
measurement at all. Only four have been re-run on sycophancy by another group: synthetic
anti-sycophancy data, pinpoint tuning, bias-augmented consistency training, and Contrastive
Activation Addition. For eight rows, our full-text reading found numbers that conflict within the
source paper. And no method was validated against an adaptive adversary: every evaluation applies
scripted pressure, the non-adversarial class of Section~\ref{subsec:adversaryclasses}.

\subsection{Data and Training Tier}
\label{subsec:training}

The most durable interventions act before deployment. Adding synthetic examples in which
sycophantic responses are marked incorrect reduces sycophancy on held-out
prompts~\citep{wei2023synthetic}, an approach extended to decoder-only architectures
by \citet{wang2024synthetic}, re-run as a baseline by three other
groups~\citep{chen2024pinpoint,beigi2025smart,obrien2026neurons}, and carried to adversarial
dialogue by Pressure-Tune~\citep{zhang2025pressure}. Precision matters as much as data: Supervised
Pinpoint Tuning fine-tunes only the fewer than 5\% of attention heads that drive sycophancy,
raising the truthfulness of Llama-2-13B by 67.83 points with 168 million tunable parameters, where
naive fine-tuning on the same objective costs 8.57 points of GSM8K accuracy~\citep{chen2024pinpoint}.
CAUSM removes spurious user-preference correlations through causally motivated head
reweighting~\citep{li2025causm}, SMART trains on truthful trajectories found by uncertainty-aware
tree search~\citep{beigi2025smart}, and reward decomposition splits the RL reward into
pressure-resistance and evidence-responsiveness terms~\citep{mohsin2026pressure}; all three grade A.
Consistency training makes behavior on prompts with and without the user's cue
alike~\citep{irpan2025consistency}, and its rate-matching variant is the one intervention tested
against displacement (Section~\ref{subsec:appraisal})~\citep{imran2026rmct}. Direct Preference
Optimization on anti-sycophancy pairs reduces sycophancy in six independent
studies~\citep{li2024mmsy,tan2025duetpd,atwell2025basil,gallego2024rdpo,wang2026dual,lai2026tddpo},
with grades from A to D, and it exposes the over-correction risk of every resistance control: on a
vision-language model, DPO cut sycophancy from 94.6\% to 5.4\% while acceptance of valid user
corrections fell from 98.6\% to 1.7\%~\citep{li2024mmsy}. Objectives that also reward accepting
genuine corrections~\citep{tan2025duetpd,wang2026dual} are the remedy.

\subsection{Inference Tier: Prompting, Steering, Monitoring}
\label{subsec:inference}

Inference-time controls deploy on existing systems without weight updates, exploiting the framing
sensitivity of Section~\ref{subsec:prompt_sensitivity}. Asking the model to convert a user's
assertion into a question before answering reduces sycophancy more than instructing it not to be
sycophantic~\citep{dubois2026askdont}. A neutral third-person perspective reduces sycophancy by up
to 63.8\% in multi-turn debate, measured as turns before the first flip, but its effect is mixed on
false presuppositions and degrades as conversations lengthen~\citep{hong2025sycon}, exactly the
regime the threat model flags. Prompt-level defenses now reach medical images~\citep{xu2025viper},
code review~\citep{fahad2026codesmell}, agent gating~\citep{shah2026silicon}, and multi-agent
debate, where giving agents priors about which peers are sycophancy-prone raises final accuracy by
10.5 points~\citep{kasprova2026polite}. They need no model access, which makes them the only
option for closed APIs, but two thirds of the black-box methods grade C or below.

White-box access enables steering. Contrastive Activation Addition adds a vector derived from
labeled sycophantic and non-sycophantic contrast pairs~\citep{panickssery2023steering}; independent
re-runs confirm reductions on other model families~\citep{kelkar2026persona,chen2026pca}, but its
original multiple-choice effect is small and not monotonic in the steering direction and was
tested on one model family, so it grades C. PCA-guided scaling makes steering monotonic across
three model families~\citep{chen2026pca}, linear probes in attention activations modulate
sycophancy without degrading factual accuracy~\citep{genadi2026linear}, and the anchor localization
of \citet{duszenko2026anchors} suggests control could target the generation steps where agreement
is committed. For reasoning models, MONICA monitors per-step drift and calibrates it away
mid-inference across 12 datasets and three models~\citep{hu2025monica}.

\subsection{Governance Tier and Human Oversight}
\label{subsec:policy}

Technical controls persist only if evaluation is institutionalized. Development pipelines should
measure sycophancy the way bias and hallucination are measured, comparing outputs with and without
user-belief context~\citep{fanous2025syceval}, with AEDI providing a scalar suitable for
longitudinal tracking across model versions~\citep{botas2026aedi}. High-stakes deployments should
be required to report multi-turn adversarial results (SYCON, Pressure-Tune) and single-turn
factual audits (SycEval) in model cards and release criteria~\citep{hong2025sycon,
zhang2025pressure,fanous2025syceval}, and because the four reference types of
Section~\ref{subsec:reftypes} certify different things, a release criterion should name its type.
In sensitive domains, systems should be restricted from unqualified agreement, with human
professional oversight when input suggests clinical risk.

Oversight obligations follow from the consequence evidence. Sycophancy erodes epistemic autonomy,
shaping beliefs toward the user's priors while raising trust~\citep{turner2026programmed,
cheng2025prosocial,ibrahim2026warm}. Deployers serving vulnerable populations, where safety
interventions occur in fewer than one third of delusional-content
turns~\citep{auyeung2025psychogenic} and compliance with clinically false requests reaches
100\%~\citep{chen2025helpfulness}, carry a duty of care to demonstrate sycophancy resistance under
social and authority pressure, not merely to pass static content classifiers. Transparency is the
last layer: disclosures, visible disagreement indicators, and interface guidance are legitimate
controls at the interaction layer.

\subsection{Coverage: Controls Against the Threat Model}
\label{subsec:coverage}

Table~\ref{tab:coverage} crosses the control tiers with the channels of
Section~\ref{sec:threatmodel}. Three structural gaps stand out. First, coverage of premise and
document assertions is thin rather than absent: A-CC measures compliance with assertions supplied by
tools as well as users~\citep{waqas2025acc}, and two retrieval-side controls target
belief-confirming evidence~\citep{ravishankara2025fvarag,liu2026cormrag}, but neither reports a
sycophancy measurement, so the channel is coded partial. Instructions embedded in documents are
indirect prompt injection (Section~\ref{sec:injection}); their defenses lie outside our corpus, so
that row is marked out of scope rather than undefended. Second, covert sycophancy is only beginning
to be measured~\citep{han2026implicit,hong2026visual}, and the one control validated against
displacement addresses unverbalized cue influence~\citep{imran2026rmct}, not selective framing in
open-ended advice, so full circles in the overt rows still overstate assurance against covert
variants. Third, the strongest cells depend on white-box access or retraining, leaving deployments
on closed third-party APIs with prompting and monitoring alone.

